<Project>
  <!--
    Single-evaluation sentinel. This body is packed ONLY to buildTransitive\, and
    build\Microsoft.UI.Reactor.targets is a one-line forwarder that imports it —
    because NuGet flows build\ to a DIRECT PackageReference only, so an app that
    gets Reactor through an intermediate library needs the buildTransitive\ copy or
    it compiles against the DSL with no generator and no REACTOR_SOURCEMAP, and
    every CallSite is silently null.

    One body, imported once, rather than two copies with a skip guard. A guard does
    not work here: MSBuild evaluates every PropertyGroup in the file before any
    ItemGroup, so a property set by the "second" import is already visible when the
    FIRST import's items are evaluated — measured, and it suppressed the wiring
    entirely rather than just the duplicate. The forwarder reads this property in an
    <Import Condition>, which IS evaluated in order, so it is skipped when NuGet has
    already imported this file directly.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <_MicrosoftUIReactorTargetsLoaded>true</_MicrosoftUIReactorTargetsLoaded>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Spec 010 — source mapping.

    Mirrors the platform precedent rather than inventing one. WPF gates XAML
    source info on <XamlDebuggingInformation>, which defaults TRUE in Debug and
    FALSE in Release; WinUI 3's Live Visual Tree / XAML Hot Reload likewise
    require a Debug configuration. So source mapping follows the build
    configuration, not a bespoke property the app author has to discover.

    Debug  → generated. The interceptors are compiled in, but stamp nothing
             until Reactor.Diagnostics.ReactorSourceMap.Enabled is set, which
             ReactorApp does when the process is launched with the devtools
             verb. A Debug build that never runs devtools pays no runtime
             allocation (measured: byte-identical per-render bytes to a build
             with no generator at all).
             Release, BY DEFAULT, not generated. No build cost, and no absolute source paths
             are baked into shipped binaries. This is a default, not a prohibition: the
             analyzer below keys on $(ReactorSourceMap) alone, so an explicit
             <ReactorSourceMap>true</ReactorSourceMap> DOES generate interceptors in Release
             and DOES embed mapped source paths in the binary. Only opt in for a Release
             build you are not distributing.

    Cost of the Debug default, measured on samples/ReactorGallery (9,272 lines,
    1,660 intercepted call sites): +0.99s on an incremental one-line rebuild,
    +2.18s on a clean compile. Scales at roughly 0.5-0.6 ms per intercepted call
    site, so set <ReactorSourceMap>false</ReactorSourceMap> explicitly on a very
    large app if the edit loop matters more than element attribution.

    The InterceptorsNamespaces line below is not optional: emitting an
    [InterceptsLocation] into a compilation that has not opted the namespace in
    is CS9137, a hard build error rather than a silent no-op. It is welded to the
    same condition so the two can never drift apart, and so no app author ever
    has to know the namespace exists.
  -->
  <!--
    Debug default. NOTE the empty-string arm: this file is imported by the repo's
    own Directory.Build.props at the TOP of a project, and $(Configuration) is not
    defaulted until the SDK props are imported later, so it is genuinely EMPTY
    here for any build that does not pass -c / -p:Configuration explicitly (which
    is exactly what CI does). Treating empty as Debug matches MSBuild's own
    default. A real Release build passes Configuration as a global property, so it
    is already set by this point and correctly declines.

    A PackageReference consumer imports this file after the project body, where
    $(Configuration) is always set, so the empty arm is inert for them.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ReactorSourceMap)' == '' and ('$(Configuration)' == 'Debug' or '$(Configuration)' == '')">
    <ReactorSourceMap>true</ReactorSourceMap>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Wire the generator and its compile-time signal TOGETHER, and only for a
    PackageReference consumer (the DLL sits under buildTransitive\sourcemap\ in the
    package, not analyzers\dotnet\cs, precisely so it is not auto-loaded).

    Two reasons this is conditional rather than always-on:

      * Cost. An analyzer under analyzers/dotnet/cs loads into EVERY consumer
        compilation, and this generator's syntax provider visits every
        invocation to classify it semantically. Gating the DLL's presence is
        what actually delivers the "Release costs nothing" claim; gating only
        the emit still pays the whole discovery pass.

      * Honesty of the signal. REACTOR_SOURCEMAP means "interceptors are
        compiled into this assembly". Defining it anywhere the generator is not
        actually running would make guarded inspector code treat an unavailable
        provider as a broken one. The constant is therefore defined in the same
        conditional block that adds the analyzer, and never on its own.

    In-repo projects do not go through this path; they consume Reactor by
    project reference, so they add the generator ProjectReference and the
    constant themselves. Only the handful that opt in get either.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ReactorSourceMap)' == 'true' and Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)sourcemap\Reactor.SourceMap.Generator.dll')">
    <Analyzer Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)sourcemap\Reactor.SourceMap.Generator.dll" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ReactorSourceMap)' == 'true' and Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)sourcemap\Reactor.SourceMap.Generator.dll')">
    <InterceptorsNamespaces>$(InterceptorsNamespaces);Microsoft.UI.Reactor.Generated</InterceptorsNamespaces>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);REACTOR_SOURCEMAP</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Lets the generator read $(ReactorSourceMap) via the analyzer config. -->
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="ReactorSourceMap" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      ReactorApplication.InitializeComponent loads XAML resources from these
      sidecar assets. ProjectReference consumers get them from the library
      output automatically, but PackageReference publish only copies assemblies
      unless the package target explicitly marks these files for output/publish.

      There is deliberately NO Reactor.pri item here (issue #1271). The package no
      longer ships one: it carried a single EmbeddedData copy of the .xbf below, and
      any .pri that reaches the consumer's layout — whether via lib/ or via a
      CopyToOutputDirectory item like these — is handed to the Windows App SDK's
      `makepri.exe Dump` expansion, whose $(IntermediateOutputPath) output path
      breaks past MAX_PATH on deep trees.

      The loose files below are what satisfy InitializeComponent once the .pri is gone.
      Measured: the consuming app's own generated .pri then contains NO Reactor entry at
      all, and the app still resolves ms-appx:///Reactor/Hosting/ReactorApplication.xaml
      — for an unpackaged app that URI falls back to the file on disk beside the
      executable. Either sidecar alone is sufficient (the .xaml is parsed when the .xbf
      is absent); both ship so the compiled path stays available.
    -->
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\lib\**\Reactor\Hosting\ReactorApplication.xaml"
          Link="Reactor\Hosting\ReactorApplication.xaml"
          TargetPath="Reactor\Hosting\ReactorApplication.xaml"
          CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest"
          CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest"
          Visible="false" />
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\lib\**\Reactor\Hosting\ReactorApplication.xbf"
          Link="Reactor\Hosting\ReactorApplication.xbf"
          TargetPath="Reactor\Hosting\ReactorApplication.xbf"
          CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest"
          CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest"
          Visible="false" />

    <!-- Default: devtools subsystem is trimmed out of AOT publishes.
         Apps that need devtools override by adding their own
         <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="Reactor.DevtoolsSupport"
                                          Value="true" Trim="true" />.
         SDK uses last-write-wins for duplicate Include keys on
         RuntimeHostConfigurationOption. -->
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="Reactor.DevtoolsSupport"
                                    Value="false"
                                    Trim="true"
                                    Condition="'@(RuntimeHostConfigurationOption->WithMetadataValue('Identity', 'Reactor.DevtoolsSupport'))' == ''" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="_NormalizeReactorDevtoolsSupportSwitch"
          BeforeTargets="_GenerateRuntimeConfigurationFilesInputCache;GenerateBuildRuntimeConfigurationFiles;PrepareForILLink">
  <ItemGroup Condition="'@(RuntimeHostConfigurationOption->WithMetadataValue('Identity', 'Reactor.DevtoolsSupport')->WithMetadataValue('Value', 'true'))' != ''">
      <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Remove="Reactor.DevtoolsSupport" />
      <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="Reactor.DevtoolsSupport"
                                      Value="true"
                                      Trim="true" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <Target Name="_RestoreReactorDevtoolsSupportRuntimeKnob"
          AfterTargets="PrepareForILLink"
          BeforeTargets="WriteIlcRspFileForCompilation"
          Condition="'@(_TrimmerFeatureSettings->WithMetadataValue('Identity', 'Reactor.DevtoolsSupport')->WithMetadataValue('Value', 'true'))' != ''">
  <ItemGroup>
      <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="Reactor.DevtoolsSupport"
                                      Value="true" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    Publish fix for WindowsAppSDK#6394.

    An UNPACKAGED (WindowsPackageType=None) WinUI 3 app generates its resource index
    ($(TargetName).pri) and its compiled XAML (*.xbf) into the BUILD output, but nothing
    copies them into the PUBLISH output — the MakePRI publish stage is gated on
    AppxPackage=true. The published app then starts with no resource index, so
    XamlControlsResources loads as an empty dictionary and MRT lookups fail
    (TabView/NavigationView throw FileNotFoundException out of
    ResourceAccessor::GetLocalizedStringResource). Measured on the stock `reactorapp`
    template: the published app dies at startup with 0xC000027B
    (STATUS_APPLICATION_INTERNAL_EXCEPTION) inside native XAML, and dropping the app's
    own .pri back into the publish folder makes it run.

    This target lives here, in the targets Reactor SHIPS, rather than in each app,
    because every Reactor app hits it. It supersedes the per-project
    `_CopyWinUIResourcesForAot` snippet that samples used to carry: that one was gated on
    PublishAot=true, but the gap is not AOT-specific — a plain `dotnet publish -c Release`
    loses the .pri too.

    Opt out with <ReactorCopyWinUIResourcesToPublish>false</ReactorCopyWinUIResourcesToPublish>.

    NOT version-gated, so do not wait for an SDK bump to delete this. A first-party fix
    already exists — MSIX.Pri.targets' AddProjectPriToResolvedFileToPublish /
    AddXbfFilesToResolvedFileToPublish — but it is reachable only via
    EnableMsixTooling -> MsixPackageSupport -> ShouldImportMsixCommonTargets ->
    Packaging.targets -> Pri.targets. With the flag off the build takes the MrtCore
    branch, which has no publish step. Measured: those targets are present in both the
    MSIX build tools a current app resolves (1.7.251221100) and the newest published
    (1.7.260903100); MsixPackageSupport is assigned in exactly one place, conditioned
    solely on EnableMsixTooling; and no Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK* package sets it. The
    stock template on the newest stable SDK (2.5.1 / WinUI 2.3.9 / Foundation 2.3.12)
    still publishes without its .pri and still crashes 0xC000027B when this target is
    disabled. Delete this only once that fix runs WITHOUT EnableMsixTooling; the
    opt-out control in PriPackagingTests starts failing when that happens.

    Note the Exclude: $(PublishDir) is nested INSIDE $(OutputPath) by default
    (bin\<cfg>\<tfm>\<rid>\publish\), so globbing $(OutputPath)**\*.xbf without it would
    re-collect the files a previous publish already copied and recreate them one level
    deeper on every subsequent publish.
  -->
  <Target Name="_ReactorCopyWinUIResourcesToPublish"
          AfterTargets="Publish"
          Condition="'$(ReactorCopyWinUIResourcesToPublish)' != 'false'
                     and '$(PublishDir)' != ''
                     and '$(AppxPackage)' != 'true'
                     and '$(WindowsPackageType)' != 'MSIX'
                     and ('$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' or '$(OutputType)' == 'WinExe')">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_ReactorWinUIPublishResource Include="$(OutputPath)**\*.xbf"
                                    Exclude="$(PublishDir)**\*.xbf" />
      <_ReactorWinUIPublishResource Include="$(ProjectPriFullPath)"
                                    Condition="'$(ProjectPriFullPath)' != '' and Exists('$(ProjectPriFullPath)')" />
      <_ReactorWinUIPublishResource Include="$(OutputPath)$(AssemblyName).pri"
                                    Condition="'$(ProjectPriFullPath)' == '' and Exists('$(OutputPath)$(AssemblyName).pri')" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(_ReactorWinUIPublishResource)"
          DestinationFiles="@(_ReactorWinUIPublishResource->'$(PublishDir)%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)')"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>
</Project>
